# Pattern composition

The GUI is a front end for SuperCollider pattern code. It is an enhancement of that code. Composition is ordinary pattern code: `Pseq`, `Ppar`, `Prand`, and the other standard pattern classes.

The document is the saved record. Evaluating it builds the pattern objects. The Arranger follows those objects. Nested chip positions are derived from the pattern and are not written to `Seq().arrange`.

## Referencing a named sequence

`Seq().pat(id)` returns a `SeqPat`. A `Pseq` or `Ppar` can embed that object. A bare symbol cannot: the list pattern would yield the symbol instead of playing the sequence. `SeqPat` stores the sequencer and the id, and looks the sequence up when it embeds, so a later evaluation of the leaf is what plays.

`Seq().compose(id, pattern)` stores a `\composition` sequence with no synth id. `Seq().addMidiSequence` remains the registration for one MIDI part on one synth. Leaves stay on the Arranger canvas as well as inside a composition.

`Seq().play(id)` embeds `pat(id)`. `SeqPat` is the only lookup. A MIDI leaf goes through one wrapper: default `\amp` and `\timingOffset`, the MIDI route and note-off callback, that leaf's pre- and post-key sets, and `\lag` copied from `\timingOffset`. A composition is embedded unwrapped, so each inner `SeqPat` wraps its own MIDI leaf. A missing id throws. A cycle throws.

## Display

`PatternStructure` walks the live pattern. `Ppar` and `Ptpar` stack children vertically. `Pchain` stacks its patterns vertically. Every other `ListPattern` lays children out in a row, and the chip label is the class name. A `FilterPattern` is a chip around one child. A numeric repeat other than 1, including `inf`, is drawn on the label and is not unrolled. `Pbind` is a leaf; its keys are not opened. A `SeqPat` that names a MIDI sequence is a leaf labeled with the id. A `SeqPat` that names a composition expands that composition unless the id is already on the walk path.

Only `\composition` sequences are walked. A MIDI sequence stays a single label chip.

## Out of scope

Do not treat `Seq().pat` as the value of a `Pbind` key. That asks a whole sequence for one event key. This work does not walk inside `Pbind`.

Loading a pattern back into the piano roll or drum grid is a later iteration. Only an atomic pattern, the shape `WriteCommand` writes for one named sequence on one synth, would be editable there. A composition is not.

Drum-grid patterns are ordinary named leaves once `WriteCommand` has stored them. This work does not extend `DrumProgrammer`. `PatternText` and `SequencerGui_Old` stay retired.
